    "q-94998": {
      "dom_id": "step-q94998",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "Which curve is defined by the equation ${x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}=6?$",
      "worked_solution": "Consider the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}.$ To determine the curve defined by the given equation, we reduce $Q(x)$ to its canonical (diagonal) form. \n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of $Q(x),$ which is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we find the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =2,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our quadratic form can be reduced to $2{y}_{1}^{2}.$ \n\nIn the new variables, the given curve has the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{y}_{1}^{2} & =6 \\\\ {y}_{1}^{2} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis represents the two parallel lines ${y}_{1}=\\sqrt{3}$ and ${y}_{1}=-\\sqrt{3}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
