    "q-94985": {
      "dom_id": "step-q94985",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "Which curve is defined by the equation $9{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2}=10?$",
      "worked_solution": "Consider the quadratic form $Q(x)=9{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2}.$ To determine the curve defined by the given equation, we reduce $Q(x)$ to its canonical (diagonal) form.  \n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of $Q(x),$ which is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we find the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}9-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-15\\lambda +50 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =5,\\,10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our quadratic form can be reduced to $5{y}_{1}^{2}+10{y}_{2}^{2}.$ \n\nIn the new variables, the curve has the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{y}_{1}^{2}+10{y}_{2}^{2} & =10 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{1} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}}+\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{1} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse with semiaxes $\\sqrt{2}$ and $1.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
