    "q-95011": {
      "dom_id": "step-q95011",
      "name": "Question 10",
      "problem": "Which curve is defined by the equation $24{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+21{x}_{2}^{2}=100?$",
      "worked_solution": "Consider the quadratic form $Q(x)=24{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+21{x}_{2}^{2}.$ To determine the curve defined by the given equation, we reduce $Q(x)$ to its canonical (diagonal) form.  \n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of $Q(x),$ which is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}24 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 21\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we find the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}24-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 21-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-45\\lambda +500 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =25,\\,20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, our quadratic form can be reduced to $25{y}_{1}^{2}+20{y}_{2}^{2}.$  \n\nIn the new variables, the curve has the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25{y}_{1}^{2}+20{y}_{2}^{2} & =100 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{5} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{5})^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse with semiaxes   $2$ and $\\sqrt{5}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
