    "q-140551": {
      "dom_id": "step-q140551",
      "name": "Question 11",
      "problem": "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{aligned}]$ and the quadratic curve ${x}^{T}\\,Ax=11.$ Given that $A$ has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-5,$ find the principal axes of the curve.",
      "worked_solution": "Notice that $A$ is symmetric. \nThe principal axes of the curve \n\n$$\n{x}^{T}\\,Ax=11\n$$\n\nare the vectors that give an orthonormal eigenvector basis for $A.$ Let's now find these eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-5I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-5)I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{aligned}]+5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-5)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the principal axes of our curve are\n\n$\\,$ $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
