    "q-140552": {
      "dom_id": "step-q140552",
      "name": "Question 18",
      "problem": "Find the principal axes of the curve $7{x}_{1}^{2}+8{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-8{x}_{2}^{2}=9.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, consider the quadratic form $Q(x)=7{x}_{1}^{2}+8{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-8{x}_{2}^{2}.$ The matrix of the form is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is symmetric. The principal axes of the curve \n\n$$\n{x}^{T}\\,Ax=9\n$$\n\nare the vectors that form an orthonormal eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\nBy solving the characteristic equation $|A-\\lambda I|=0,$ we obtain\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=8\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-9.$\n\nLet's now find the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-8I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -8\\end{aligned}]-8[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -16\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-8I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{17},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-9,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-9)I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -8\\end{aligned}]+9[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}16 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A+9I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{17},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the principal axes of our curve are\n\n$\\,$ $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
